WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.234
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C two, Book three.

2
00:00:05.534 --> 00:00:09.118
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.418 --> 00:00:15.809
Unit eighteen: Technology Policy. Today you will propose action using address / tackle + noun phrase.

4
00:00:16.109 --> 00:00:18.796
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:19.733 --> 00:00:23.013
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:23.850 --> 00:00:25.304
Technology policy.

7
00:00:26.317 --> 00:00:32.217
Technology policy means government decisions on how technology is developed and used.

8
00:00:32.817 --> 00:00:36.244
Technology policy shapes who benefits from innovation.

9
00:00:36.850 --> 00:00:40.036
Your turn. Say: Technology policy.

10
00:00:42.900 --> 00:00:44.551
Technology policy.

11
00:00:45.317 --> 00:00:46.504
Regulation.

12
00:00:47.517 --> 00:00:50.480
Regulation means an official rule governing conduct.

13
00:00:51.083 --> 00:00:53.663
Regulation of platforms is hotly debated.

14
00:00:54.250 --> 00:00:57.193
Your turn. Say: Regulation.

15
00:01:00.033 --> 00:01:01.301
Regulation.

16
00:01:02.050 --> 00:01:03.620
Innovation incentive.

17
00:01:04.617 --> 00:01:08.485
Innovation incentive means a reward that encourages new inventions.

18
00:01:09.083 --> 00:01:11.501
Patents are an innovation incentive.

19
00:01:12.117 --> 00:01:15.164
Your turn. Say: Innovation incentive.

20
00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:19.628
Innovation incentive.

21
00:01:20.383 --> 00:01:21.733
Digital divide.

22
00:01:22.717 --> 00:01:27.479
Digital divide means the gap between those with and without access to technology.

23
00:01:28.083 --> 00:01:30.826
The digital divide left rural students offline.

24
00:01:31.417 --> 00:01:33.814
Your turn. Say: Digital divide.

25
00:01:36.667 --> 00:01:37.993
Digital divide.

26
00:01:38.750 --> 00:01:40.053
Data privacy.

27
00:01:41.050 --> 00:01:45.697
Data privacy means the right to control how personal data is collected and used.

28
00:01:46.283 --> 00:01:48.503
Data privacy laws limit tracking.

29
00:01:49.117 --> 00:01:52.384
Your turn. Say: Data privacy.

30
00:01:55.233 --> 00:01:56.571
Data privacy.

31
00:01:57.317 --> 00:01:58.457
Antitrust.

32
00:01:59.450 --> 00:02:03.818
Antitrust means law that prevents monopolies and unfair competition.

33
00:02:04.417 --> 00:02:07.113
Antitrust action targeted the dominant platform.

34
00:02:07.717 --> 00:02:10.311
Your turn. Say: Antitrust.

35
00:02:13.167 --> 00:02:14.377
Antitrust.

36
00:02:15.117 --> 00:02:16.687
Emerging technology.

37
00:02:17.683 --> 00:02:22.214
Emerging technology means new technology whose effects are not yet fully known.

38
00:02:22.817 --> 00:02:25.745
Emerging technology often outpaces the law.

39
00:02:26.350 --> 00:02:29.792
Your turn. Say: Emerging technology.

40
00:02:32.633 --> 00:02:34.377
Emerging technology.

41
00:02:35.117 --> 00:02:36.617
Governance framework.

42
00:02:37.617 --> 00:02:41.810
Governance framework means a system of rules and oversight for managing something.

43
00:02:42.417 --> 00:02:45.717
A governance framework set clear duties for developers.

44
00:02:46.317 --> 00:02:49.491
Your turn. Say: Governance framework.

45
00:02:52.333 --> 00:02:53.927
Governance framework.

46
00:02:54.733 --> 00:02:59.046
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:59.883 --> 00:03:06.634
Visal, my cousin can't join online classes because his village has no reliable internet. Whose problem is that?

48
00:03:07.750 --> 00:03:14.768
It's a policy problem. Policymakers must address the growing digital divide, or inequality will deepen.

49
00:03:15.883 --> 00:03:17.778
What else should governments tackle?

50
00:03:18.883 --> 00:03:30.754
Data privacy, for one. Regulators must tackle the collection of personal data without consent, and antitrust authorities should address market concentration.

51
00:03:31.850 --> 00:03:34.047
Won't regulation slow innovation?

52
00:03:35.150 --> 00:03:42.516
It can. A good governance framework must address risks while preserving innovation incentives.

53
00:03:43.667 --> 00:03:45.446
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:46.283 --> 00:03:49.885
Grammar focus: Address and tackle plus noun phrase.

55
00:03:50.185 --> 00:03:51.500
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:52.183 --> 00:03:55.321
Policymakers must address the growing digital divide.

57
00:03:56.217 --> 00:03:59.157
Regulators should tackle unauthorised data collection.

58
00:04:00.050 --> 00:04:03.872
A governance framework must confront risks without stifling innovation.

59
00:04:04.833 --> 00:04:12.304
address / tackle / confront / deal with + problem. No preposition after address or tackle: address the divide (not 'address to the divide').

60
00:04:13.050 --> 00:04:17.525
Be careful! Don't say: Leaders must address to the divide.

61
00:04:19.467 --> 00:04:21.965
Say: Leaders must address the divide.

62
00:04:22.265 --> 00:04:24.137
address + direct object.

63
00:04:24.933 --> 00:04:27.412
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:28.200 --> 00:04:29.677
Governing Technology.

65
00:04:29.977 --> 00:04:34.717
Technology policy must keep pace with innovation that often moves faster than law.

66
00:04:35.700 --> 00:04:43.888
Policymakers must address the growing digital divide, because those without access to the internet are excluded from education, work and public services.

67
00:04:44.900 --> 00:04:52.229
Regulators should tackle data privacy and market concentration, since a few firms control vast amounts of personal information.

68
00:04:53.233 --> 00:05:00.573
Emerging technologies such as artificial intelligence raise further questions of safety, accountability and fairness.

69
00:05:01.567 --> 00:05:06.713
A sound governance framework protects the public while preserving the incentives that drive innovation.

70
00:05:07.800 --> 00:05:11.616
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:12.450 --> 00:05:15.494
Policymakers must blank the growing digital divide.

72
00:05:15.794 --> 00:05:19.750
A, address. B, address to. C, address at.

73
00:05:23.300 --> 00:05:25.300
The answer is A: address.

74
00:05:26.450 --> 00:05:29.994
The gap between those with and without access to technology is the blank.

75
00:05:30.294 --> 00:05:34.319
A, antitrust. B, digital divide. C, incentive.

76
00:05:37.867 --> 00:05:40.365
The answer is B: digital divide.

77
00:05:41.517 --> 00:05:43.830
Law that prevents monopolies is blank.

78
00:05:44.130 --> 00:05:49.153
A, antitrust. B, privacy. C, regulation only.

79
00:05:52.700 --> 00:05:54.967
The answer is A: antitrust.

80
00:05:56.117 --> 00:05:57.780
Choose the correct sentence.

81
00:05:58.080 --> 00:06:05.042
A, Governments must tackle the problem.. B, Governments must tackle to the problem.. C, Governments must tackle at problem..

82
00:06:08.600 --> 00:06:11.888
The answer is A: Governments must tackle the problem..

83
00:06:13.050 --> 00:06:17.232
According to the reading, a sound framework protects the public while preserving…

84
00:06:17.532 --> 00:06:22.265
A, incentives to innovate. B, monopolies. C, inequality.

85
00:06:25.800 --> 00:06:28.624
The answer is A: incentives to innovate.

86
00:06:29.833 --> 00:06:33.252
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:34.117 --> 00:06:38.310
What is one technology policy challenge you think governments should address?

88
00:06:55.117 --> 00:06:56.571
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:56.871 --> 00:07:23.442
Governments should address data privacy, because companies collect huge amounts of personal information, often without people really understanding what they have agreed to. Clear rules, strong enforcement and plain-language consent would give individuals more control. This protects trust in digital services, which innovation ultimately depends on.

90
00:07:23.742 --> 00:07:25.486
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:26.383 --> 00:07:31.323
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and address and tackle plus noun phrase.

92
00:07:31.623 --> 00:07:37.096
Today's value is digital responsibility. Use technology in ways that protect others.

93
00:07:37.396 --> 00:07:40.014
See you in Unit nineteen. Goodbye!

94
00:07:40.314 --> 00:07:43.303
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

